from (values
('113.44.d.8.C',3,'The Tenth Amendment says that powers not given to the national government are reserved to whom?','["the president", "foreign nations", "the states or the people", "the courts"]'::jsonb,2,'It supports states'' rights.','original'),
('113.44.d.8.C',4,'Which conflict illustrates a dispute over states'' rights versus national authority?','["the Cold War", "World War I", "the Spanish-American War", "the Civil War"]'::jsonb,3,'Secession was challenged by the Union.','original'),
('113.44.d.8.D',3,'The Constitution limits state power by saying what?','["federal law is supreme over conflicting state law", "states can coin money", "states can overrule federal law", "states may declare war"]'::jsonb,0,'The Supremacy Clause settles conflicts.','original'),
('113.44.d.8.D',3,'Which of these can neither a state nor the national government do?','["establish schools", "pass an ex post facto law", "collect taxes", "build roads"]'::jsonb,1,'Both are banned from punishing acts retroactively.','original'),
('113.44.d.9.A',3,'Which method fills a public office by selection by someone in authority?','["recall", "referendum", "appointment", "election"]'::jsonb,2,'The president appoints cabinet members.','original'),
('113.44.d.9.A',3,'Federal judges are filled through which method?','["hereditary succession", "lottery", "direct popular election", "appointment"]'::jsonb,3,'The president nominates; the Senate confirms.','original'),
('113.44.d.9.B',3,'The Electoral College has how many electors in total?','["538", "270", "435", "100"]'::jsonb,0,'A candidate needs 270 to win.','original'),
('113.44.d.9.B',4,'A candidate wins the presidency by winning at least how many electoral votes?','["300", "270", "218", "538"]'::jsonb,1,'That is a majority of 538.','original'),
('113.44.d.9.B',3,'In most states, all electoral votes go to whom?','["the loser", "the House Speaker", "the candidate who wins the state''s popular vote", "the Supreme Court"]'::jsonb,2,'Most states use winner-take-all.','original'),
('113.44.d.9.C',3,'The 17th Amendment changed how senators are chosen. How are they selected now?','["by the Supreme Court", "by state legislatures", "by the president", "by direct popular vote"]'::jsonb,3,'Before 1913, legislatures chose them.','original'),
('113.44.d.10.A',3,'Which is a function of political parties?','["recruiting and nominating candidates", "enforcing laws", "writing court decisions", "printing money"]'::jsonb,0,'Parties organize campaigns.','original'),
('113.44.d.10.A',3,'A primary election is held to do what?','["set taxes", "choose a party''s candidate for the general election", "appoint judges", "elect the final winner"]'::jsonb,1,'Parties use primaries and caucuses.','original'),
('113.44.d.10.B',3,'The United States has a two-party system, dominated by which two parties?','["Federalists and Whigs", "Libertarians and Reform", "Democrats and Republicans", "Socialists and Greens"]'::jsonb,2,'Single-member districts favor two parties.','original'),
('113.44.d.10.B',4,'A role of third parties in U.S. elections is often to do what?','["control the Senate", "choose Supreme Court justices", "win most seats", "raise issues that major parties later adopt"]'::jsonb,3,'They influence debate.','original'),
('113.44.d.11.A',3,'A constitutional republic differs from an absolute monarchy because it does what?','["limits power by law and elects representatives", "gives power to a king", "abolishes courts", "ends elections"]'::jsonb,0,'Law and representation constrain rulers.','original'),
('113.44.d.11.A',3,'In a totalitarian state, which statement is true?','["citizens elect all leaders freely", "the government controls nearly all aspects of life", "the press is free", "courts are independent"]'::jsonb,1,'Compare with limited government.','original'),
('113.44.d.11.B',4,'A presidential system differs from a parliamentary system because in a presidential system the executive is what?','["chosen by the legislature", "appointed by the courts", "separately elected and independent of the legislature", "a monarch"]'::jsonb,2,'In parliamentary systems the legislature selects the prime minister.','original'),
('113.44.d.11.B',4,'A potential disadvantage of presidential systems is what?','["no checks on power", "frequent no-confidence votes", "too much unity", "gridlock when president and legislature are of different parties"]'::jsonb,3,'Divided government can slow action.','original'),
('113.44.d.12.A',3,'How does limited government help protect individual rights?','["it restricts officials to powers granted by law", "it removes all laws", "it ends the courts", "it gives officials unlimited power"]'::jsonb,0,'The rule of law and limits on power guard against abuse.','original'),
('113.44.d.12.B',3,'The Declaration of Independence names which three unalienable rights?','["property, taxes and trade", "life, liberty and the pursuit of happiness", "speech, press and religion", "voting, jury and bearing arms"]'::jsonb,1,'Unalienable means they cannot be taken away.','original'),
('113.44.d.12.C',3,'The First Amendment protects which freedoms?','["bearing arms and quartering", "voting, jury and counsel", "religion, speech, press, assembly and petition", "bail and due process"]'::jsonb,2,'These five freedoms are in the First Amendment.','original'),
('113.44.d.12.C',3,'The Fourth Amendment protects against what?','["cruel punishment", "quartering of soldiers", "double jeopardy", "unreasonable searches and seizures"]'::jsonb,3,'Warrants require probable cause.','original'),
('113.44.d.12.C',3,'The Fifth Amendment includes protection against what?','["being forced to testify against yourself", "unreasonable searches", "excessive bail", "cruel punishment"]'::jsonb,0,'It also protects due process and bans double jeopardy.','original'),
('113.44.d.12.C',3,'The Sixth Amendment guarantees what?','["protection from quartering", "a speedy and public trial by jury and the right to counsel", "the right to bear arms", "freedom of religion"]'::jsonb,1,'It protects the accused.','original'),
('113.44.d.12.C',3,'The Eighth Amendment prohibits what?','["self-incrimination", "quartering of troops", "cruel and unusual punishments", "unreasonable searches"]'::jsonb,2,'It also bans excessive bail and fines.','original'),
('113.44.d.12.C',3,'The Second Amendment concerns what right?','["the right to a jury", "freedom of speech", "freedom of the press", "the right to keep and bear arms"]'::jsonb,3,'It has been interpreted by the Supreme Court.','original'),
('113.44.d.12.D',3,'The Founders protected religious freedom partly because many colonists had what experience?','["fled religious persecution in Europe", "worshipped only one faith", "were all the same denomination", "ruled by a state religion in America"]'::jsonb,0,'Many settlers sought freedom of conscience.','original'),
('113.44.d.12.E',3,'In Tinker v. Des Moines, the Court held that students have what right?','["unlimited speech anywhere", "to symbolic speech at school unless it disrupts learning", "to disobey teachers", "no speech rights at school"]'::jsonb,1,'The Court protected black armbands worn in protest.','original'),
('113.44.d.12.E',3,'In Gideon v. Wainwright, the Court ruled that states must do what?','["allow school prayer", "abolish segregation", "provide a lawyer for defendants who cannot afford one", "end the death penalty"]'::jsonb,2,'It extended the Sixth Amendment right to counsel.','original'),
('113.44.d.12.E',3,'Brown v. Board of Education (1954) declared what unconstitutional?','["flag salutes", "loyalty oaths", "school prayer", "racial segregation in public schools"]'::jsonb,3,'It overturned the ''separate but equal'' idea in schools.','original'),
('113.44.d.12.F',3,'Due process of law means that government must do what?','["follow fair procedures before taking life, liberty or property", "punish quickly", "ignore evidence", "act in secret"]'::jsonb,0,'It appears in the Fifth and Fourteenth Amendments.','original'),
('113.44.d.12.F',3,'The Miranda warning informs suspects of what?','["the charges against others", "their right to remain silent and to an attorney", "the trial date", "the judge''s name"]'::jsonb,1,'Miranda v. Arizona required it.','original'),
('113.44.d.13.A',3,'Serving on a jury is an example of what?','["a way to avoid taxes", "a privilege only for the wealthy", "a duty that may require personal sacrifice for the common good", "an optional hobby"]'::jsonb,2,'Citizens set aside personal time for justice.','original')
) as v(code, diff, prompt, choices, ans, expl, src)
join public.teks_items i on i.code = v.code and i.is_standard
join public.teks_documents d on d.id = i.document_id and d.subject = 'Social Studies' and d.language = 'en'
on conflict (fingerprint) do nothing;
